      <workList xml:id="workDesc_fb41bd38">
         <work xml:id="work_idbdd0063f">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_2cbe7cc0">A6:001</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id7b7865b9" xml:lang="it">Concerto pro clavicembal del Sigr. Vivaldi</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_573bb9f1">
               <persName role="arranger" xml:id="persName_idadae3268">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_534ac951">Antonio Vivaldi</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id7bd46c1e">
               <date enddate="1735" startdate="1727" xml:id="date_13e0d6ad">1727–35</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_idf4716ea6">Leipzig,</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_idc23a441a">
               <p xml:id="idm69">While Scheibe was still living in Leipzig – that is, before 1735 when he started on his tour which ended in Hamburg – he made at least one transcription of a Vivaldi concerto for solo harpsichord (in A major for two violins and orchestra, op. 3 no. 5 (RV 519)). There are indications that Scheibe must have studied with <persName xml:id="idm70" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Johann Sebastian Bach</persName> who employed such transcriptions in his teaching (see also SchW A1:013). The paper on which the present work has been written is the same as that Bach used in his cantata ‘Die Zeit und Jahre macht’ composed in his Cöthen period (1717–23). It should be noted that the Breitkopf catalogue of 1761 lists two concerti by <persName xml:id="idm71" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Antonio Vivaldi</persName> transcribed for harpsichord. The catalogue indicates Bach as the arranger, but it is possible that one of the transcribers was Scheibe. How it ended up in the Breitkopf collection of house manuscripts is an open question. Perhaps it was through the cantor of Neukirche in Leipzig, <persName xml:id="idm72" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Carl Gotthelf Gerlach</persName>, or possibly Scheibe himself. Since the Breitkopf catalogues list Bach as arranger of the works the publishers might have acquired the collection, including Scheibe's arrangement, from Bach.</p>
            </history>
            <langUsage xml:id="langUsage_7d46d76a">
               <language xml:id="en">English</language>
               <language xml:id="it">Italian</language>
            </langUsage>
